description:
Two BFS implementations in src/memory.rs use HashSet<String> for visited-set deduplication, requiring a String allocation (.as_ref().to_owned()) on every visit:

1. src/memory.rs:583-584 (get_ancestors):
   let mut visited: std::collections::HashSet<String> =
       ids.iter().map(|id| id.as_ref().to_owned()).collect();
   ...
   let key = parent_id.as_ref().to_owned();
   if visited.contains(&key) { continue; }
   visited.insert(key);

2. src/memory.rs:632-633 (get_descendant_ids):
   let mut visited: std::collections::HashSet<String> =
       std::iter::once(id.as_ref().to_owned()).collect();
   ...
   let child_key = node.id.as_ref().to_owned();
   if visited.insert(child_key) { ... }

What idiomatic Rust would do: HashSet<jmap_types::Id> directly. Id derives Hash + Eq + Clone (verified — see crate-jmap-types/src/id.rs and the use site at backend.rs:150 below). Use HashSet<Id> and insert id.clone() (which is a refcount bump if Id is Arc-backed, or a String clone of the same cost as .to_owned() but without the intermediate &str step).

What goes wrong as-is: every BFS visit does one String allocation just to populate the HashSet key. For get_descendant_ids over an N-node tree, that's N allocations on the hot path. The Drop of the HashSet later releases all of them. This is the kind of gratuitous-clone-to-escape-the-borrow-checker pattern the supplement specifically flags under 'ownership'.

The inconsistency is the strongest signal: the same crate's src/backend.rs:150 (the FileNodeBackend::query_subtree default impl) does it correctly:
   let mut seen: std::collections::HashSet<jmap_types::Id> =
       root_ids.iter().cloned().collect();

Two BFS impls in memory.rs, one BFS impl in backend.rs, three of them, only one keys by Id. Inconsistent within the crate.

Canonical comparison: jmap-mail-server uses HashSet<Id> exclusively (no HashSet<String> for id dedup). Filenode-specific divergence.

Propagation note: local to this crate. Fix is mechanical: change HashSet<String> to HashSet<Id>, remove the .as_ref().to_owned() at insert/lookup sites, use id.clone() instead.
